WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.980
Secure logging starts by deciding which events are worth recording.

2
00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:15.000
Authentication events are usually important: successful sign-ins, failed attempts, MFA changes, account recovery, lockouts, and suspicious patterns.

3
00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:26.700
Authorization decisions can also matter, especially denied access to sensitive resources, administrative actions, privilege changes, and attempts to cross tenant or ownership boundaries.

4
00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:32.720
These records help teams understand whether controls are working and where misuse may be happening.

5
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:37.000
Administrative actions and privileged operations deserve strong logging.

6
00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:49.660
Role changes, permission grants, configuration updates, secret rotations, feature flag changes, data exports, account disablement, and changes to security settings are the kinds of events investigators often need later.

7
00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:59.600
Data access events may be important for sensitive records or regulated workflows, particularly when access is unusual, high volume, or tied to privileged support activity.

8
00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:06.020
The goal is to log security-relevant decisions and outcomes, not every byte of data.

9
00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:08.860
Operational failures are also useful.

10
00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:22.080
System errors, dependency failures, background job failures, payment workflow errors, queue processing problems, and business-critical workflow failures can become security-relevant during an incident.

11
00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:26.580
A failed downstream service may explain why a control did not complete.

12
00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:31.560
A spike in validation failures may indicate probing or a broken client.

13
00:01:31.660 --> 00:01:36.540
Good logging helps teams connect application behavior to security and operational reality.
